1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,786
Book Three, Proposition Thirteen.

2
00:00:02,786 --> 00:00:10,169
A circle does not touch a circle at more points than one, whether it touch it internally or externally.

3
00:00:10,169 --> 00:00:19,130
For, if possible, let the circle A B D C touch the circle E B F D, first internally, at more points than one, namely B and D.

4
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:26,517
Let the center G of circle A B D C, and the center H of circle E B F D, be taken.

5
00:00:26,517 --> 00:00:37,440
By Three Eleven, the straight line joined from G to H and produced falls on the points of internal contact B and D, as B G H D.

6
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:42,649
Because G is the center of circle A B D C, the radius B G equals G D.

7
00:00:42,649 --> 00:00:50,283
Since G D contains H D, B G is greater than H D, and therefore the whole segment B H is much greater than H D.

8
00:00:50,283 --> 00:00:55,974
Yet H is the center of circle E B F D, so B H also equals H D—which is impossible.

9
00:00:55,974 --> 00:01:00,415
Thus two circles cannot touch internally at more than one point.

10
00:01:00,415 --> 00:01:03,469
Nor can they touch externally at two points.

11
00:01:03,469 --> 00:01:09,368
For if circle A C K touches circle A B D C externally at A and C, join the chord A C.

12
00:01:09,368 --> 00:01:21,166
By Three Two, the chord joining two points on a circle falls within each circle; yet because the circles touch externally, A C falls outside circle A C K—which is absurd.

13
00:01:21,166 --> 00:01:28,245
Therefore, a circle does not touch a circle at more points than one, whether internally or externally.

14
00:01:28,245 --> 00:01:29,980
As was required to prove.

15
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,919
Proposition Three Thirteen completes Euclid's trilogy on tangent circles.

16
00:01:34,919 --> 00:01:47,051
Where Three Ten proved that two circles cannot intersect at more than two points, Three Thirteen proves that when intersection collapses into tangency, at most a single point of contact can exist.

17
00:01:47,051 --> 00:01:59,988
Heath contrasts Euclid's collinear-radius reductio with Robert Simson's perpendicular-bisector proof, showing that both rely on the symmetry of the line of centers established in Three Eleven and Three Twelve.

18
00:01:59,988 --> 00:02:09,520
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred twelve unites Three Two—chord interiority—with Three Eleven's line of centers across forty-two ancestor nodes.

